WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.019
The disease has already claimed more victims
than Legionnaires' disease and toxic shock

00:00:05.019 --> 00:00:06.950
syndrome combined.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:10.470
This is the deadliest outbreak of Ebola on
record.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:14.760
There are a lot of experts around the world
who do not yet know how contagious this is,

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.470
how deadly this new virus is.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.550
Here in the U.S. panic is increasing around
the world about the pandemic.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.614
As the disease spreads to North America, the
consequences are multiplying.

00:00:24.614 --> 00:00:28.400
The World Health Organization has declared
a swine flu pandemic.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.480
Is the world ready for the coronavirus?

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:42.620
Cholera, bubonic plague, smallpox, and influenza,
are some of the most deadly diseases in human history.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:47.880
And when they spread across regions of the
world, an epidemic becomes a pandemic and

00:00:47.889 --> 00:00:51.239
it can result in more deaths than wars and
natural disasters.

00:00:51.239 --> 00:00:59.180
A good example of that is the 1918-1919 influenza
pandemic, which took place just as the First

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.899
World War was ending. Some historians believe
that up to 100 million people died. In other

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.400
words, many more people than died in the war
itself.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:16.030
And when a newly identified coronavirus emerged
in China in late 2019, governments reacted

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.500
like they had in the past:

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.580
With quarantines and travel bans, while researchers
raced to create a vaccine.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:27.910
We tend to think about managing an epidemic
episode like this in terms of public health

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:32.660
or science. But in fact, what I would argue
is at the root of many of the issues here

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:39.160
are sort of social, cultural, political issues,
practices, how people live, working environments,

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.040
how people travel, why they live in cities.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.860
In 1980 some predicted that science would
overcome epidemics.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:52.820
That was the year the World Health Organization
declared one of the biggest killers in human

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.280
history was eradicated: smallpox.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.690
In its 3,000-year existence, this one disease
killed an estimated 300 million people worldwide.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:09.439
The global vaccination program had prevailed
and many virologists at the time were declaring

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.680
an end to infectious diseases.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.960
That's a sort of shining example, right,
of a great optimism that human

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.800
societies could actually put an end to disease.
At the time that smallpox was being eradicated

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.440
was precisely the time that other emerging
infectious diseases like Ebola were coming

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:28.920
onto the scene.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.980
Smallpox is still the only human disease to
be eradicated.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:38.739
And more have been popping up, making the
jump from animals to humans.

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:44.580
When people started to settle and cities developed
about 10,000 years ago, our relationship with

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:53.019
animals also changed and spillovers took place.
Like smallpox, measles, tuberculosis, influenza,

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:58.510
et cetera, originated in animal populations.
And obviously, that was a very big change

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:04.760
from hunter gatherers where there wasn't a possibility of diseases to be sustained in large populations.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:12.160
Diseases that are able to live between species
like SARS, make it nearly impossible to eradicate.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:17.700
That's why the plague, which exists in rodents
and killed between 75 to 200 million people

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.370
in Eurasia, still exists to this day.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:30.049
Ebola, MERS, SARS, rise a very complicated
interspecies relations. And they exist in

00:03:30.049 --> 00:03:36.659
reservoirs in the wild. Those diseases are
very difficult for us to tackle.

00:03:36.659 --> 00:03:40.060
Wild animals are just a small piece of the
puzzle.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:45.139
The last century saw an industrialization
of livestock, making a perfect intermediary

00:03:45.139 --> 00:03:46.989
for diseases.

00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:53.569
And in 2009 we saw the H1N1 influenza virus
make the jump from pigs to people.

00:03:53.569 --> 00:04:01.050
The world is now at the start of the 2009 influenza
pandemic.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:07.660
The U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention
estimated that as many as 575,000 people died

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:11.419
from H1N1 during the first year the virus
circulated.

00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:16.450
So when you mass produce animals and you have
an outbreak, it's very easy for the

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:20.510
for the disease to move very rapidly through
the animal populations and then linked to

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.320
all of these is bigger environmental issues: climate change – means that

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.860
diseases that could exist in some areas are
moving into other areas. We're seeing

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.490
mosquito borne diseases move into areas
where it wasn't posing a problem.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:40.120
According to the WHO, a global temperature
increases of 2 to 3ºC would increase the

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:47.960
number of people who are at risk of malaria
by around 3-5%, or several hundred million.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:51.810
Malaria already killed 405,000 people in 2018.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.930
And linked to all of this is urbanization.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.870
Millions of people, living side by side, allowing
diseases to spread rapidly.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:03.420
There are sort of environmental reasons why
diseases happen in certain places.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.920
But I think that the fact that you've got mass
cities, the fact you've got a lot of people

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.540
moving certainly helps
in the spread of disease.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:13.490
And that’s why how governments respond is
more crucial than ever.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:18.650
Critics of the coronavirus travel bans say
that it only exacerbates the outbreak:

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:23.980
shortages become more common, people suffering
from non-coronavirus related illnesses have

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:28.430
difficulty making it to hospitals, and many
flee before the measures are implemented.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:34.240
There isn’t sufficient data that shows travel
bans actually do anything to stop the spread.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.920
It's interesting that as this corona virus
outbreak is happening. We've got an H5N1 outbreak

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:48.840
in a poultry farm in China. We've got H1N1
in Taiwan. There are many other diseases around

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.990
and they could pose problems. We've had African
swine fever decimating pig population. 300

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:58.680
million pigs have died. So I think there's
an issue about what diseases gets focused

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:03.720
on why it gets focused on what the politics
of that is.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.650
What’s clear is in order to tackle infectious
diseases, a more integrated approach that

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.380
considers all of the variables is needed.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:16.170
Over the last 10, 15 years has been a move
towards one health, which is an integrated

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:21.990
health that essentially tries to put human
health and animal health together to say that

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:25.800
actually we can't deal with human health unless
we integrate and think about what's happening

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.120
in animal populations. I think we need to
go further.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:36.610
A much more integrated approach that draws
on insights about how human behavior determines

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:41.950
disease dynamics. Unless we understand
these cultural social processes. I think we're

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:49.030
not going to be in a very strong position
in the future to tackle outbreaks.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:52.870
Understanding that new outbreaks are a complex
issue that depends on more than just vaccines

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:58.310
and sanitation – that they’re interwoven
in the way we live, and consume, is perhaps

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:02.610
the first step in tackling pandemics.

